window.G12_DEFINITIONS = [[{"title":"Circular motion (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In circular motion, the object just moves in a circle. "}],[{"text":"For example – artificial satellites going around the earth at a constant height."}]]},{"title":"Rotational motion (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In rotational motion, the object rotates about an axis."}],[{"text":" For example  - The rotation of the earth about its own axis which creates the cycle of   day and night."}],[{"text":"  - Motion of the blades of a fan. "}],[{"text":"  - Motion of a Ferris wheel."}]]},{"title":"Rigid body","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A rigid body is a body that does not deform or change shape."}]]},{"title":"* Rotational motion under constant angular acceleration","paragraphs":[[{"text":"If the angular velocity of the rotating object changes at a constant rate, then we can say that the angular acceleration is constant and the motion is called the rotational motion under constant angular acceleration."}]]},{"title":"(Why is linear velocity in circular motion also called tangential velocity?)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Since the direction of linear velocity in circular motion is tangent to the path, the linear velocity is also called tangential velocity."}]]},{"title":"Tangential acceleration","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the magnitude of the angular velocity of a rotating object change, the magnitude of the linear velocity will also change and the object will have a tangential acceleration."}]]},{"title":"Right-hand rule","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Wrap the right hand around the axis of rotation so that the fingers are pointing in the direction of rotation. The thumb points in the direction of the angular velocity vector."}]]},{"title":"* Centripetal acceleration","paragraphs":[[{"text":" For uniform circular motion, the centripetal acceleration vector points towards the centre of the circular path while the linear velocity vector is tangential to the path. This acceleration is called centripetal acceleration."}]]}],[{"title":"Different forces which can provide centripetal force","paragraphs":[[{"text":"(i)  Tension force (T)"}],[{"text":"(ii) Gravitational force ("},{"math":true,"tex":"F_{G}","plain":"F_(G)"},{"text":")"}],[{"text":"(iii)  Electric force ("},{"math":true,"tex":"F_{e}","plain":"F_(e)"},{"text":")"}],[{"text":"(iv) Frictional force ("},{"math":true,"tex":"F_{f}","plain":"F_(f)"},{"text":")"}],[{"text":"(v) Normal force ("},{"math":true,"tex":"F_{N}","plain":"F_(N)"},{"text":")"}],[{"text":"(vi) Upward thrust (Lifting force) on a plane ("},{"math":true,"tex":"F_{L}","plain":"F_(L)"},{"text":")"}]]},{"title":"Banking of roads","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Banking of roads is raising the outer edge of the curved road higher than the inner edge. "}]]},{"title":"Angle of banking","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The angle that the inclined track makes with horizontal is called angle of banking. "}]]},{"title":"* Centripetal force (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Centripetal force is the force acting on an object in circular motion which is directed towards the axis of rotation (or) centre of curvature."}]]},{"title":"* Centrifugal force","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Centrifugal force in a circular motion is not a real force; it acts radially away from the centre of the circle. It results from the effect of inertia."}]]},{"title":"Comparison between Centripetal and Centrifugal Force (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Centripetal Force | Centrifugal Force"}],[{"text":"(i) | Centripetal force is the force acting on an object in circular motion which is directed towards the axis of rotation or centre of curvature. | (i) | Centrifugal force in a circular motion is not a real force; it acts radially away from the centre of the circle. It results from the effect of inertia."}],[{"text":"(ii) | If a car is travelling through a curve on a circular horizontal road, the centripetal force provided by the force of friction between the tires of the vehicle and the road surface allows the car to negotiate the turn. | (ii) | When a car in motion takes a sudden turn in a curved path, passengers in the car experience an outward push. This is due to the centrifugal force acting on the passengers."}],[{"text":"(iii) | A satellite orbiting a planet is supported by the centripetal force. | (iii) | Removing the excess water by spin drying clothes in a washing machine is with the help of centrifugal force."}]]},{"title":"Usage of centrifugal force (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"  Centrifuge, Centrifugal pump, Washing machine, Centrifugal governor"}]]}],[{"title":"Fluid","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Fluid is a substance that offers no permanent resistance to deforming forces. The study of fluids has two parts, statics and dynamics. "}]]},{"title":"Hydrodynamics","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The study of the flow of liquid is called hydrodynamics. "}]]},{"title":"Hydrostatic","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Hydrostatic is the study of liquid at rest. "}]]},{"title":"Streamline ငွေ့ရည်စီးကြောင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In the flow of fluids, the path or the flow of the fluid particles is called streamline"}]]},{"title":"* Laminar flow ပြေပြစ်စီးကြောင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If fluid particles are moving steady in smooth paths in layers, with each layer moving smoothly past the adjacent layers with no mixing; such a flow is called laminar flow."}],[{"text":"In this steady laminar flow, streamlines do not cross each other and every fluid particle arriving at a given point has the same velocity."}]]},{"title":"* Turbulent flow ဂယက်ထစီးကြောင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the flow or path of the fluid particles are irregular, their direction is always changing or whirling; this fluid movement is called turbulent flow. "}],[{"text":"In turbulent flow, the speed of the fluid at a point is continuously undergoing changes in both magnitude and direction. "}],[{"text":"Sea breeze is a laminar flow. Strom is a turbulent flow."}]]},{"title":"Ideal laminar flow","paragraphs":[[{"text":"The fluid is incompressible."}],[{"text":"The flow has no friction or friction is neglected. (Zero viscosity)"}],[{"text":"The fluid flow is laminar (steady) and turbulent flow is not taken into account."}]]},{"title":"* Bernoulli’s Theorem","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Bernoulli’s theorem sates that the sum of pressure energy, kinetic energy and potential energy per unit volume of an incompressible, non-viscous fluid in a streamlined laminar flow remains constant."}],[{"text":"p + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{1}{2}","plain":"(1)/(2)"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\rho ","plain":"ρ"},{"text":" v² + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\rho ","plain":"ρ"},{"text":" g h = constant"}]]},{"title":"* Viscosity (What?)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The resistance to fluid motion (or) internal resistance of fluid is called viscosity. "}]]},{"title":"Viscosity (Define)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Viscosity is defined as the ratio of shearing stress to the velocity gradient."}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\tau","plain":"τ"},{"text":" = - "},{"math":true,"tex":"η","plain":"η"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{dv}{dy}","plain":"(dv)/(dy)"},{"text":" "}],[{"text":"SI unit – pascal second (Pa s),"}],[{"text":"equivalent unit – newton second per metre squared (N s m⁻²)"}]]},{"title":"Dynamic viscosity (Newton's Law of Viscosity)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" One way is to measure fluid's resistance to flow when an external force is applied. This is known as dynamic viscosity."}]]},{"title":"Kinematic viscosity (Stokes' law of viscosity)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The other way is to measure the resistive flow of a fluid under the weight of gravity. It is known as kinematic viscosity."}]]},{"title":"Newton's law of viscosity","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Newton's law of viscosity states that the shear stress between the two adjacent layers of fluid is directly proportional to the negative value of the velocity gradient between the same two adjacent fluid layers. "}],[{"text":"In symbols, shear stress "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\tau","plain":"τ"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\propto","plain":"∝"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{dv}{dy}","plain":"(dv)/(dy)"},{"text":"  (or)  "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\tau","plain":"τ"},{"text":" = - "},{"math":true,"tex":"η","plain":"η"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{dv}{dy}","plain":"(dv)/(dy)"},{"text":" "}]]},{"title":"Shear stress","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Shear stress is the shearing force per unit area. "}],[{"math":true,"tex":"\\tau","plain":"τ"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{F}{A}","plain":"(F)/(A)"},{"text":" "}],[{"text":"Where,  F = the shearing force acting between two adjacent layers of a fluid, "}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"η","plain":"η"},{"text":" = the viscosity (coefficient of viscosity), "}],[{"text":"  A = the area of the fluid layer ,                            "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{dv}{dy}","plain":"(dv)/(dy)"},{"text":" = velocity gradient."}],[{"text":"SI unit of shear stress is newton per metre squared (N m⁻²)"}]]},{"title":"Cohesion","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Cohesion refers to forces between molecules of the same substance."}]]},{"title":"Adhesion","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Adhesion refers to forces between molecules of different substances. "}]]},{"title":"Surface tension (What is surface tension?)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Surface tension is the tendency of the surface of a liquid to behave like a stretched elastic skin (or membrane)."}]]},{"title":"* Surface tension (define)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Surface tension is defined as the force per unit length that acts across any line on a surface tending to pull the surface apart. "}],[{"math":true,"tex":"\\gamma","plain":"γ"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{F}{l}","plain":"(F)/(l)"}],[{"text":"   Where, F = force, l = length, "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\gamma","plain":"γ"},{"text":" = surface tension "}],[{"text":"The SI unit of surface tension is newton per metre (N m⁻¹). "}]]},{"title":"Capillary action (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Capillary action is defined as the spontaneous flow of a liquid into a narrow tube or porous material."}],[{"text":" Cohesion > Adhesion – capillary fall – "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\theta ","plain":"θ"},{"text":" = obtuse angle"}],[{"text":" Cohesion < Adhesion – capillary rise – "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\theta ","plain":"θ"},{"text":" = acute angle"}]]}],[{"title":"Energy resources","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Energy resources are all forms of fuel used in the modern world, either for heating, generation of electrical energy, or for other forms of energy conversion processes. "}]]},{"title":"Energy demand","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Energy demand is the term used to describe the consumption of energy by human activities."}]]},{"title":"* Non-renewable resources (conventional energy sources) (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A non-renewable resource is a natural substance that is not replenished with the speed at which it is consumed. It is a finite resource."}],[{"text":"Eg. Fossil fuels like coal, oil, natural gases, etc., nuclear energy"}]]},{"title":"* Renewable energy sources (non-conventional energy sources) (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Renewable energy sources (also called non-conventional energy sources) are continuously replenished by natural processes."}],[{"text":"Eg. Solar energy, wind energy, hydro energy, ocean thermal energy, geothermal energy, biomass"}]]},{"title":"* Solar energy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Solar energy is the radiant light and heat from the sun that has been harnessed by humans since ancient times using a range of ever-evolving technologies. "}]]},{"title":"Irradiance   ရောင်ခြည် (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The total amount of radiation energy passing through per unit area and per unit time is called irradiance and is measured in W m⁻² or k W m⁻². "}]]},{"title":"Insolation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Insolation is the solar irradiance received on a horizontal surface such as ground. "}]]},{"title":"Photovoltaic effect","paragraphs":[[{"text":" When semiconductor materials are exposed to light, some of the photons of light are absorbed by the semiconductor crystal which causes a significant number of free electrons in the crystal."}],[{"text":"    * Conversion of light energy into electrical energy is based on a phenomenon called photovoltaic effect."}]]},{"title":"Solar cell (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A solar cell (also known as a photovoltaic cell) is defined as an electrical device that converts light energy into electrical energy through the photovoltaic effect. "}]]},{"title":"Concentrated solar power (CSP) systems","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Concentrated solar power (CSP) systems generate solar power by using mirrors or lenses to concentrate a large area of sunlight onto a specific point of receiver which converts into heat."}]]},{"title":"* Wind energy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Wind energy is the energy generated or produced by harnessing the power of the wind. "}]]},{"title":"* Hydroelectric energy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The flowing water and water stored in huge dams is also a very important source of energy which is known as hydroelectric energy."}],[{"text":" Hydroelectric energy is a form of energy that harnesses the power of water in motion such as water flowing over a waterfall to generate electricity."}]]},{"title":"Penstock မြေအောက်တွင် တူးထားသည့် တူးမြောင်းတစ်မျိုး","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A penstock is a closed conduit that channels the flow of water to turbines with water flow regulated by gates and valves."}]]},{"title":"* Geothermal energy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Geothermal energy is a type of renewable energy taken from the earth’s core (the internal heat of the earth) and it is one of the oldest types of natural sources of heat. "}]]},{"title":"Biomass","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Biomass is renewable organic material that comes from plants and animals. Biomass contains stored chemical energy."}]]},{"title":"* Acid rain","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Acid rain is caused by a chemical reaction that begins when compounds like sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides are released into the air. "}],[{"text":"The energy transfers which occur in each type of energy resource."}],[{"text":"Fossil fuels:  chemical energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" thermal energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" kinetic energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy "}],[{"text":"Solar energy:  light & thermal energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy"}],[{"text":"Nuclear:  nuclear energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" thermal energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" kinetic energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy"}],[{"text":"Wind:  kinetic energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy "}],[{"text":"Bio-fuel:  chemical energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" thermal energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" mechanical energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy "}],[{"text":"Hydroelectricity:  gravitational potential energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" kinetic energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy"}],[{"text":"Geothermal:  thermal energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":"  kinetic energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy "}],[{"text":"Tidal: kinetic energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" electrical energy "}]]}],[{"title":"Heat    အပူစွမ်းအင်","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Heat is a form of energy that can be transferred from one system to another as a result of temperature difference. "}]]},{"title":"* Heat conduction","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Heat conduction is one mode of energy transfer. The individual parts of a medium do not move as a whole in heat conduction. "}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{cond}","plain":"H_(cond)"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":" A "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{T_{2} - T_{1}}{l}","plain":"(T₂ - T₁)/(l)"},{"text":" Where, "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{cond}","plain":"H_(cond)"},{"text":" = rate of heat conduction, (W)"}],[{"text":"    A = cross sectional area, (m²)"}],[{"text":"    T₂ – T₁ = temperature difference, (K) or ("},{"math":true,"tex":"℃","plain":"℃"},{"text":")"}],[{"text":"    l = length or thickness, (m)"}],[{"text":"  "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":" = thermal conductivity of the material,"}]]},{"title":"Temperature gradient အပူချိန်ပြောင်းတာနှုန်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The ratio of temperature difference to the length is called temperature gradient. "}],[{"text":" Temperature gradient = ( "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{T_{2} - T_{1}}{l}","plain":"(T₂ - T₁)/(l)"},{"text":" ).  "}]]},{"title":"* Heat current အပူစီးကြောင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The rate of heat flow (heat current) is the amount of heat energy transferred per unit time."}],[{"text":"        H = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{\\Delta Q}{t}","plain":"(ΔQ)/(t)"}],[{"text":" Where, H = heat current (or) heat rate (or) heat flow (or) heat per second ယူနစ်W (watt)"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Delta ","plain":"Δ"},{"text":"Q = amount of heat ယူနစ် J (joule)"}]]},{"title":"Fourier’s law of heat conduction","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Fourier’s law states that the rate of heat flow per unit area (heat flux density) is proportional to the magnitude of temperature gradient. "}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{H}{A} \\propto - \\frac{dT}{dx}","plain":"(H)/(A) ∝- (dT)/(dx)"}],[{"text":"6. Thermal conductivity ("},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":")  အပူလျှောက်ကူးကိန်း"}],[{"text":" The thermal conductivity of a material can be defined as the rate of heat transfer per unit area per unit temperature gradient.      "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{\\frac{H}{A}}{\\frac{(T_{2}-T_{1})}{l}}","plain":"((H)/(A))/(((T₂-T₁))/(l))"}]]},{"title":"* Heat convection","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Heat convection is the flow of heat through a fluid from places of higher temperature to places of lower temperature by movement of fluid itself."}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{conv}","plain":"H_(conv)"},{"text":" = q A (T₂ – T₁)"}],[{"text":"  Where  "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{conv}","plain":"H_(conv)"},{"text":" = the rate of heat convection,  (W)"}],[{"text":"    q = the convective heat transfer coefficient (heat convection constant)"}],[{"text":"    A = the surface area of the object being cooled or heated, (m²)"}],[{"text":"    T₂ = the temperature of the surrounding fluid, "}],[{"text":"    T₁ = the surface temperature of the object, "}],[{"text":"When T₂ > T₁  heat is gained by the object and "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{conv}","plain":"H_(conv)"},{"text":" is positive.   (T₂ – T₁ = +)"}],[{"text":"When T₂ < T₁  heat is lost by the object and "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{conv}","plain":"H_(conv)"},{"text":" is negative.        (T₂ – T₁ = -)"}]]},{"title":"* Heat radiation","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Heat radiation is a transfer of heat that does not require a material medium. Heat is transferred by thermal radiation which are electromagnetic waves that pass through a medium or even vacuum."}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{rad}","plain":"H_(rad)"},{"text":" = e "},{"math":true,"tex":"σ","plain":"σ"},{"text":" A T⁴ Where,  "},{"math":true,"tex":"H_{rad}","plain":"H_(rad)"},{"text":" = rate of heat radiation, (W)"}],[{"text":"    A = surface area,  (m²)"}],[{"text":"    T = absolute temperature (K) "}],[{"text":"    e = the emissivity,"}],[{"text":"e = 1 for black body e < 1 for other bodies, "}]]},{"title":"* Blackbody","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The object which can absorb all the electromagnetic radiations falling upon it is called a blackbody."}]]},{"title":"* Blackbody radiation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The radiation emitted by a blackbody is called blackbody radiation. "}]]},{"title":"Total emissive power စုစုပေါင်း ဖြာထွက်မှုစွမ်းအား","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The total emissive power is defined as the total radiant energy of different wavelengths emitted from unit area of a surface of a body in one second."}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"ε_{0}= \\frac{\\frac{\\Delta Q}{t}}{A}","plain":"ε₀= ((ΔQ)/(t))/(A)"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{H}{A}","plain":"(H)/(A)"}]]},{"title":"Stefan-Boltzmann’s law","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The total emissive power of a blackbody is directly proportional to the fourth power of absolute temperature. "}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"ε_{0}\\propto ","plain":"ε₀∝"},{"text":" T⁴,           "},{"math":true,"tex":"ε_{0}","plain":"ε₀"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"σ","plain":"σ"},{"text":" T⁴"}],[{"math":true,"tex":"σ","plain":"σ"},{"text":" = Stefan’s constant, "},{"math":true,"tex":"σ","plain":"σ"},{"text":" = 5.685 "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\times ","plain":"×"},{"text":" 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴"}]]},{"title":"Emissivity ထုတ်လွှတ်မှုကိန်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Emissivity is defined as the ratio of the total emissive power of an object to that of the blackbody, at the same temperature and wavelength."}],[{"text":" e = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{ε}{ε_{0}}","plain":"(ε)/(ε₀)"},{"text":". e = emissivity"}],[{"text":"  "},{"math":true,"tex":"ε","plain":"ε"},{"text":" = the total emissive power of an object  (W m⁻²)"}],[{"text":"  "},{"math":true,"tex":"ε_{0}","plain":"ε₀"},{"text":" = the total emissive power of a black body (W m⁻²)"}]]},{"title":"Thermodynamics","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Thermodynamics is the study of systems involving energy in the form of heat and work."}]]},{"title":"The Zeroth law of thermodynamics","paragraphs":[[{"text":" When two objects are separately in thermodynamic equilibrium with a third object, they all are in thermodynamic equilibrium with each other."}]]},{"title":"First law of thermodynamics","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The change in internal energy "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Delta ","plain":"Δ"},{"text":"U of a system is equal to the net heat flow Q into the system minus the total work done W by the system."}],[{"text":" In symbols,   "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Delta ","plain":"Δ"},{"text":"U = Q – W"}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Delta ","plain":"Δ"},{"text":"U = change in internal energy, Q = the net heat flow, W = work done"}]]},{"title":"Internal energy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The internal energy U of a system is defined as the sum of energies of all the constituent particles."}]]},{"title":"Thermodynamics process","paragraphs":[[{"text":"A thermodynamics process may be defined as a change from one equilibrium state of a system to another."}]]},{"title":"Quasi-static process","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In thermodynamics, a quasi-static process (also known as a quasi-equilibrium process) is a thermodynamic process that happens slowly enough for the system to remain in internal physical thermodynamic equilibrium."}]]},{"title":"* Heat Engine","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Heat engine is a system that converts heat to mechanical energy, which can then be used to mechanical work. It operates continuously and only heat and work may pass across its boundary."}]]},{"title":"Cycle","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A series of processes in which a system is brought back to its initial state is called a cycle."}],[{"text":"22. * Thermal efficiency for heat engine "},{"math":true,"tex":"η","plain":"η"}],[{"text":"Thermal efficiency for a heat engine is the ratio of work output to heat input."}],[{"text":" Thermal efficiency  =  "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{work output}{heat input}","plain":"(work output)/(heat input)"}],[{"text":"                             "},{"math":true,"tex":"η","plain":"η"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{W}{Q_{H}}","plain":"(W)/(QH)"}]]},{"title":"Reversible process","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In a reversible process, the system undergoing "}],[{"text":"the process can be returned to its initial condition along the same path on the p-V diagram, and "}],[{"text":"every point along this path is in an equilibrium state. "}]]},{"title":"Carnot’s theorem","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Carnot’s theorem states that no engine operating between two heat reservoirs can be more efficient than a Carnot engine operating between the same reservoirs."}],[{"text":"  "},{"math":true,"tex":"η_{C}","plain":"ηC"},{"text":" = 1 - "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{T_{C}}{T_{H}}","plain":"(TC)/(TH)"}]]}],[{"title":"Sound","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Sound is a sequence of pressure waves which propagates through compressible media such as air or water."}],[{"text":"2. Intensity level of sound ("},{"math":true,"tex":"β","plain":"β"},{"text":")"}],[{"text":" The level of intensity of a sound is measured relative to a reference intensity value I₀, taken as 10⁻¹² W m⁻² which correspond to the minimum intensity of sound that can be heard (threshold of hearing or minimum audible intensity)."}],[{"text":"Speed of sound in solid > speed of sound in liquid > speed of sound in gas"}],[{"text":"Threshold of hearing = minimum audible intensity = 10⁻¹² W m⁻² = 0 dB"}],[{"text":"Threshold of pain = maximum audible intensity = 120 dB"}],[{"text":"SI unit of intensity of sound is watt per metre squared (W m⁻²)."}],[{"text":"SI unit of intensity level of sound is decibel (dB)."}]]},{"title":"Echoes","paragraphs":[[{"text":"An echo is repetition of sound due to the reflection of sound."}]]},{"title":"Echolocation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Echolocation is the special ability of emitting sounds and interpreting the echoes."}]]},{"title":"Reverberation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A reverberation is a reflection of light or sound waves, or a wide reaching impact of an action."}],[{"text":"Eg. A reverberation is the sound bouncing around in a large speaker."}]]},{"title":"* Audibility","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Audibility means the ability to be heard or capability of detecting sounds in certain range of frequencies."}]]},{"title":"Range of audibility","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The range of frequencies, a person can hear is known as the range of audibility. "}]]},{"title":"Infrasound","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Low frequency sounds which are under the lower limit of audibility (20 Hz) are called infrasound and cannot be heard by human."}]]},{"title":"Ultrasound","paragraphs":[[{"text":" High frequency sounds which are above the upper limit of audibility (20 000 Hz) are called ultrasound which cannot be heard by human ear. "}]]},{"title":"Various applications of ultrasound","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Quality control ထုတ်လုပ်ပစ္စည်း၏အရည်အသွေးကိုထိန်းထားနိုင်ရန် စစ်ဆေးမှုပြုလုပ်ခြင်း","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Prenatal Ultrasound test သန္ဓေသားကို စစ်ဆေးခြင်း","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Ultrasonic scalers in dentistry သွားဆေးခန်းတွင် သွားသန့်ရှင်းရေးလုပ်ခြင်း","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Ultrasonic cleaning  ultrasound ဖြင့် သန့်ရှင်းရေးပြုလုပ်ခြင်း","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Sonar (so, n, a, r) အသံလှိုင်းဖြင့်ရေအောက်အရာဝတ္တုတို့၏တည်နေရာနှင့် အနက်ကိုတိုင်းခြင်း","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Doppler effect or Doppler shift (2024, 2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The Doppler effect or Doppler shift is the change in frequency of a wave related to an observer due to the relative motion between the source and observer."}],[{"text":"receding – redshift. approaching – blueshift."}]]},{"title":"Application of Doppler Effect (2025)","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Red shift","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Radar (ra, d, a, r)","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Medical Applications - angiodynography, echocardiography.","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"*  Timber","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Timber is the quality of sound which allows us to distinguish between different sound sources producing sound at the same pitch and loudness."}],[{"text":"The pitch of note (sound) is determined by its frequency."}],[{"text":"The loudness of the note (sound) depends upon the amplitude of the wave that produces it."}],[{"text":"Timber or quality of a sound depends on its waveform."}]]},{"title":"Intensity of sound","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The intensity of sound at a place is the energy transmitted per second per unit area held normally to direction of sound."}]]}],[{"title":"Camera","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A camera is an optical instrument that forms and records an image of an object."}]]},{"title":"The essential features of camera","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Convex lens : A convex lens focuses the light from the object to form a real inverted image on the film or electronic sensor."}],[{"text":" Diaphragm : A diaphragm is placed behind the convex lens to control the amount of light energy falling on the film."}],[{"text":" Aperture : The aperture is a circular opening through which the light passes. It can be made wide or narrow by adjusting the diaphragm."}],[{"text":"  The size or the diameter of the aperture is expressed in terms of f-numbers. The f-number of a camera is the ratio of the focal length of the lens to the diameter of aperture."}],[{"text":" Shutter : A shutter controls the time during which light enters the camera through the lens."}],[{"text":" Focusing Ring : A camera consists of a focusing ring by which the distance between the lens and the film can be adjusted to obtain a sharp image on the film."}],[{"text":" Film : The film at the back of the camera is actually a screen which receives the image. The film consists of a thin layer of chemical compounds of silver."}],[{"text":"bright light - narrow aperture – large f-number,  dim light- wide aperture – small f-number"}],[{"text":"higher the shutter speed – shorter the exposure time "}]]},{"title":"The main features of human eye","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Eyelid : Since the eyelid opens and closes the eye, it is similar to the lens cover of the camera."}],[{"text":" Cornea : The front portion of the eye is covered by a tough, transparent membrane called cornea."}],[{"text":" Aqueous humour : The region between the cornea and the eyelens is filled with a transparent liquid called the aqueous humour."}],[{"text":" vitreous humour : The region between the eyelens and the retina contains a transparent liquid called the vitreous humour."}],[{"text":" Iris : The iris which is the colored portion of the eye controls the amount of light energy entering the eye."}],[{"text":" Pupil : The pupil is in the centre of the iris and the light passes through it. It is similar to the aperture in the camera. The iris adjusts the size of the pupil in the eye.   "}],[{"text":" Ciliary muscles : The ciliary muscles are attached to the eyelens. The ciliary muscles can vary the thickness of the eyelens, thus alter the focal length of the lens to obtain a sharp image on the retina.   "}],[{"text":" Retina : The retina is the light sensitive area of the cells, at the back of the eye. The retina consists of rods and cones."}],[{"text":"   The yellow spot which is the most light-sensitive spot on the retina is used for viewing very small objects. Since the yellow spot consists of cones exclusively, the small objects can be seen clearly."}],[{"text":"   There is a spot on the retina which has no rods or cones. When an image is formed at this spot it cannot be seen at all. This spot is called the blind spot where the optic nerve enters the eye."}],[{"text":"   The light energy falling on the retina is transmitted to the brain by means of the optic nerve."}],[{"text":" bright light – pupil contract, dim light – pupil dilate"}],[{"text":" near objects – contract ciliary muscles – more convex eyelens"}],[{"text":" distant objects – relax ciliary muscles – less convex eyelens"}],[{"text":"The cornea,  မျက်လုံးအပြင်မှန် ယိုင်ညွှန်းကိန်းမှာ 1.351"}],[{"text":"The aqueous humour,  မျက်စိအရှေ့ ရည်ကြည် ယိုင်ညွှန်းကိန်းမှာ 1.337"}],[{"text":"The eyelens and  မျက်စိ မှန်ဘီလူး ပျမ်းမျှယိုင်ညွှန်းကိန်းမှာ 1.437"}],[{"text":"The vitreous humour  မျက်စိအနောက် ရည်ကြည် ယိုင်ညွှန်းကိန်းမှာ 1.337"}],[{"text":"  are refracting substances. တို့သည့် အလင်းယိုင်ခြင်းဖြစ်စေသည့် ပစ္စည်းများဖြစ်ကြသည်။"}],[{"text":"Eye  Camera"}],[{"text":"Eyelid = lens cover - open and close "}],[{"text":"Iris = diaphragm - to control the amount of light energy entering"}],[{"text":"Pupil = aperture - circular opening through which the light passes"}],[{"text":"Ciliary muscles = focusing ring - to obtain a sharp image on the retina"}],[{"text":"Retina  =  film - to receive the image"}]]},{"title":"* Farsightedness အနီးမှုန် (သို့မဟုတ်) အဝေးမြင်","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Farsightedness is a defect of vision in which distant objects are seen clearly, but objects nearby are blurry."}]]},{"title":"* Nearsightedness အဝေးမှုန် (သို့မဟုတ်) အနီးမြင်","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Nearsightedness is a defect of vision in which near objects are seen clearly, but objects far away are blurry."}]]},{"title":"* Microscope","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A microscope is an optical instrument which uses lenses to make enlarged images of objects that are too small for the unaided eye to see."}]]},{"title":"Magnifying power (MP) of a compound microscope အဏုကြည့်ကိရိယာ၏ ချဲ့အား","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The magnifying power (MP) of a compound microscope is the product of the magnification of the objective and the magnifying power of the eyepiece."}]]},{"title":"Electron microscope","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Electron microscope is a microscope with high magnification and resolution, employing electron beams in place of light and using electrons lenses."}]]},{"title":"* Telescope အဝေးကြည့်ကိရိယာ (တယ်လီစကုပ်)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" An optical instrument used for viewing distant objects is called a telescope."}]]},{"title":"Magnifying power (MP) of the refracting astronomical telescope","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The magnifying power (MP) of the refracting astronomical telescope is defined as the ratio of the angle "},{"math":true,"tex":"β","plain":"β"},{"text":" substended by the image seen by the eye through the optical instrument to the angle "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\alpha ","plain":"α"},{"text":" subtended by the object as seen without instrument."}]]},{"title":"Binocular","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A binocular is an optical instrument which consist of a pair of telescopes one for each eye, and those telescopes employ the total reflecting prisms. "}]]},{"title":"Optic","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Optic is the branch of physics that studies the nature and properties of light."}]]},{"title":"* Laser","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A laser, acronym for Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation, is a source of a narrow beam of intense, monochromatic, coherent light in the visible, infrared or ultraviolet part of the electromagnetic radiation."}],[{"text":"Two essential process to initiate a laser action are"}],[{"text":"(i) stimulated emission and "}],[{"text":"(ii) population inversion"}]]},{"title":"Fibre optics","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Fibre optics refers to the technology that transmits information as light pulses along a glass or plastic fibre."}],[{"text":"core, cladding, plastic buffer."}],[{"text":"refractive index of core slightly greater than refractive index of cladding."}],[{"text":"The fibre optics works on the principle of total internal reflection."}]]},{"title":"Endoscopy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In medicine, endoscopy is a non-surgical procedure to examine the interior of a hollow organ or cavity of the human body."}]]},{"title":"Spectrometer","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A spectrometer is a scientific instrument used to separate and measure spectral components of a physical phenomenon."}]]},{"title":"Photometry","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Photometry is the science of measuring visible light in units that are weighted according to the sensitivity of human eye."}],[{"text":"Human eye is more sensitive to green light of wavelength 550 nm,"}],[{"text":"less sensitive to blue light."}],[{"text":"18. Luminous flux  "},{"math":true,"tex":"Φ","plain":"Φ"}],[{"text":" Luminous flux (luminous power) "},{"math":true,"tex":"Φ","plain":"Φ"},{"text":" is a measurement of the power of visible light produced by a light source."}]]},{"title":"Luminous intensity  Iv","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Luminous intensity Iᵥ is a measure of the luminous flux emitted by a light source in a direction per unit solid angle."}],[{"text":"20. Solid angle  "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Omega ","plain":"Ω"},{"text":" "}],[{"text":" A solid angle "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Omega ","plain":"Ω"},{"text":" is a measure of the amount of the field of view from some particular point that a given object covers."}],[{"text":"The solid angle subtended at the centre by the whole surface area of the sphere is 4"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":" sr."}]]},{"title":"steradian","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A steradian can be defined as the solid angle subtended at the centre of a unit spheres by a unit area on its surface."}]]},{"title":"Luminance Lv","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Luminance Lᵥ is the luminous intensity per unit area of light travelling in a given direction."}]]},{"title":"Illuminance Ev","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Illuminance Eᵥ is the total luminous flux incident on a surface per unit area."}]]},{"title":"The inverse square law of illuminance","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The illuminance Eᵥ at any point on a surface is inversely proportional to the square of distance r between the source and surface. This is known as inverse square law of illuminance."}],[{"text":" Eᵥ "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\propto ","plain":"∝"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{1}{r^{2}}","plain":"(1)/(r²)"},{"text":"  "}],[{"text":" Eᵥ = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{1}{r^{2}}","plain":"(1)/(r²)"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\times ","plain":"×"},{"text":" constant "}],[{"text":" Eᵥ r² = constant"}]]}],[{"title":"Huygens’ Principle","paragraphs":[[{"text":"လှိုင်းမျက်နှာစာအနားသတ်  ထပ်ဆင့်လှိုင်းငယ်များ Huygens’ Principle states that every point on a wavefront may be considered as a source of secondary wavelets. The new position of wavefront is the envelope of the wavelets."}]]},{"title":"* Wavefront","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Wavefront is defined as a surface associated with a propagating wave which is a locus of all points in the wave that have the same phase."}],[{"text":"Wavefront is always perpendicular to the direction of propagation."}]]},{"title":"* Phase (Phase Angel)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The angular position of a point of a periodic wave is known as the phase angle."}]]},{"title":"Path difference","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Path difference indicates the distance between two points measured along the direction of propagation of the wave through the medium."}],[{"text":"In phase | Out of phase"}],[{"text":"phase difference 0, 2"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":", 4"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":", 6"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":", …,\npath difference 0, "},{"math":true,"tex":"λ","plain":"λ"},{"text":", 2"},{"math":true,"tex":"λ","plain":"λ"},{"text":", 3"},{"math":true,"tex":"λ","plain":"λ"},{"text":", …, | phase difference "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":", 3"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":", 5"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\pi ","plain":"π"},{"text":", …,\npath difference "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{λ}{2}","plain":"(λ)/(2)"},{"text":", "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{3 λ}{2}","plain":"(3 λ)/(2)"},{"text":", "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{5 λ}{2}","plain":"(5 λ)/(2)"},{"text":", …,"}]]},{"title":"* Interference of light waves","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Interference of light waves is defined as the modification in the distribution of light energy (intensity) when two or more waves superimpose each other."}],[{"text":"If the path difference is equal to an internal number of wavelengths (0, "},{"math":true,"tex":"λ","plain":"λ"},{"text":", 2"},{"math":true,"tex":" λ","plain":" λ"},{"text":", …,) the waves will interfere constructively, leading to a bright fringe on the screen."}],[{"text":"If the path difference is equal to a half-integral number of wavelengths ("},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{λ}{2}","plain":"(λ)/(2)"},{"text":", "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{3λ}{2}","plain":"(3λ)/(2)"},{"text":", "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{5λ}{2}","plain":"(5λ)/(2)"},{"text":", …,) the waves will interfere destructively, leading to a dark fringe on the screen."}]]},{"title":"* Coherent sources","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the waves emitted from two sources have the same frequency and constant phase difference, the sources are coherent sources. "}]]},{"title":"* Monochromatic","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Monochromatic light means that it has a single wavelength."}]]},{"title":"Central bright line","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The bright fringe at centre of the screen is called central bright line."}]]},{"title":"Fringe width","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Distance between two adjacent bright (or dark) fringes is called the fringe width."}]]},{"title":"* Diffraction","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Diffraction is defined as the bending of waves around the corners of an obstacle (or) through an aperture into the region of geometrical shadow of the obstacle (or) aperture."}]]},{"title":"Fresnel diffraction: ဖရက်ရှနဲ ဒစ်ဖရက်ရှင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the source of light and the screen are at a finite distance from the obstacle causing the diffraction, the diffraction is called Fresnel diffraction."}]]},{"title":"Fraunhofer diffraction: ဖရွန်ဟိုဖာ ဒစ်ဖရက်ရှင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the source of light and the screen are at an infinite distance from the obstacle causing the diffraction, the diffraction is called Fraunhofer diffraction. "}]]},{"title":"* Diffraction grating","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A diffraction grating is an optical device which consists of a large number of parallel slits of same width separated by equal opaque spaces."}]]},{"title":"Grating constant (N)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The number of lines (or slits) per unit length (line per cm) is called the grating constant. "}]]},{"title":"Reflection grating","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A reflection grating is a shiny surface having many fine grooves."}],[{"text":"Eg. compact disc (CD)"}]]}],[{"title":"* Capacitor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A capacitor is an electrical device that stores electrical energy in the form of an electric field."}]]},{"title":"Description of a capacitor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A capacitor consists of two conductors separated by a small distance. An insulator is inserted between its conductors."}],[{"text":"Polarized capacitors – have polarities (positive and negative terminals)"}],[{"text":"Non-polarized capacitors – do not have polarities"}]]},{"title":"Film Capacitors (Non-polarized capacitors)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Film capacitors are the ones that use plastic film as the dielectric medium. "}],[{"text":"Working voltages up to 1500 V. "}],[{"text":"Tolerance from 10 "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\%","plain":"%"},{"text":" to 0.01 "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\%","plain":"%"},{"text":" "}]]},{"title":"Ceramic Capacitors (Non-polarized capacitors)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Ceramic capacitors are the ones that use ceramic as the dielectric material. "}],[{"text":"Used - audio frequency ("},{"math":true,"tex":"a","plain":"a"},{"text":"f) to radio frequency (rf)"}]]},{"title":"Electrolytic Capacitors (polarized capacitors)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Electrolytic capacitors are the ones that use the oxide layer as the dielectric material."}],[{"text":"have a wide tolerance capacity."}],[{"text":"There are mainly two types of electrolytic capacitors; tantalum and aluminum. "}],[{"text":"working voltages of up to approximately 500 V. "}],[{"text":"hazardous, resulting in an explosion or fire."}]]},{"title":"Variable Capacitors (polarized capacitors)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Variable Capacitors mostly use air as the dielectric medium. The capacitance can be mechanically adjusted."}],[{"text":"used in radio tuning applications."}]]},{"title":"Supercapacitors (polarized capacitors)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"A supercapacitor (SC), also called an ultracapacitor, is a high-capacity capacitor with a capacitance value much higher than other capacitors, but with lower voltage limits, that bridges the gap between electrolytic capacitors and rechargeable batteries."}],[{"text":"Supercapacitors can accept and deliver charge much faster than batteries."}]]},{"title":"Charge of the capacitor, Q","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The magnitude of the charge on each conductor, Q, is called the charge of the capacitor."}]]},{"title":"Potential difference of the capacitor, V","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The potential difference between two conductors of the capacitor, V, is called the potential difference of the capacitor."}]]},{"title":"Capacitance of a capacitor, C","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The capacitance of a capacitor is defined as the ratio of the charge to the potential difference of the capacitor."}],[{"text":" C = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{Q}{V}","plain":"(Q)/(V)"},{"text":"      Where, C = capacitance of the capacitor,  Q = charge of the capacitor,"}],[{"text":"    V = potential difference between two conductors of the capacitor. "}],[{"text":"When the charge on the capacitor is increased, the potential difference also increases proportionally. Therefore, the capacitance of a capacitor remains constant."}],[{"text":"In the SI system, the unit of capacitance is farad (F)."}]]},{"title":"One Farad (1 F)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the potential difference of the capacitor is 1 V when it is given a charge 1 C, its capacitance is 1 C V⁻¹ (or) 1 F."}],[{"text":"Therefore 1 F is equivalent to 1 C V⁻¹. "}],[{"text":"The unit farad is named in hour of Michael Faraday. "}],[{"text":"The sub-multiple units of farad are               ယူနစ်ခွဲများ"}],[{"text":"1 microfarad ("},{"math":true,"tex":"μ","plain":"μ"},{"text":"F) = 10⁻⁶ F,  "}],[{"text":"1 nanofarad (nF) = 10⁻⁹ F,  "}],[{"text":"1 picofarad (pF) = 10⁻¹² F"}]]},{"title":"Electric capacity of the conductor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The amount of charge given to a conductor to change its potential by one unit is called the electric capacity of the conductor."}]]},{"title":"* Parallel-plate capacitor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A parallel-plate capacitor is the simplest capacitor. It consists of two parallel metal plates separated by air or other insulating material."}]]},{"title":"Charging a capacitor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Charging a capacitor means connecting the capacitor to a battery."}]]},{"title":"Surface charge density","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The surface charge density is the amount of charge per unit surface area on each plate of a capacitor."}],[{"text":" In symbol, "},{"math":true,"tex":"σ= \\frac{Q}{A}","plain":"σ= (Q)/(A)"},{"text":"  Where "},{"math":true,"tex":"σ=","plain":"σ="},{"text":" surface charge density"}],[{"text":"  Q = amount of charge, A = surface area"}]]},{"title":"Dielectric constant (with respect to permittivity ratio)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The dielectric constant of an insulating material is the ratio of its permittivity to that of vacuum."}]]},{"title":"* Dielectric constant (with respect to capacitance ratio)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The ratio of the capacitance of a capacitor with an insulating material between its two conductors to the capacitance of that capacitor with a vacuum between its two conductors is called the dielectric constant of that insulating material."}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ= \\frac{C}{C_{0}}","plain":"κ= (C)/(C₀)"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":" = dielectric constant of the insulating material"}],[{"text":"  C = capacitance of a capacitor with an insulating material"}],[{"text":"  C₀ = capacitance of the capacitor with a vacuum"}],[{"text":"For vacuum or air, "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":" = 1, for other insulting material "},{"math":true,"tex":"κ","plain":"κ"},{"text":" > 1"}],[{"text":"Dielectric provide the following advantages"}],[{"text":"Increase in capacitance,"}],[{"text":"Increase in maximum operating voltage,"}],[{"text":"Possible mechanical support between the plates, which allows the plates to be close together without electrical contact, thereby decreasing d and increasing C."}]]},{"title":"Capacitance of capacitors in parallel","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The equivalent capacitance of the capacitors connected in parallel is the sum of the capacitances of the individual capacitors."}],[{"text":" Cₚ = C₁ + C₂ + …. Cₚ = n C V တူ"}]]},{"title":"Capacitance of capacitors in series","paragraphs":[[{"text":" When the capacitors are connected in series the reciprocal of the equivalent capacitance is the sum of the reciprocals of their individual capacitances."}],[{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{1}{C_{s}}","plain":"(1)/(Cs)"},{"text":"= "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{1}{C_{1}}","plain":"(1)/(C₁)"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{1}{C_{2}}","plain":"(1)/(C₂)"},{"text":" + ….  Cs = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{C}{n}","plain":"(C)/(n)"},{"text":"  Q တူ"}]]}],[{"title":"* Electromotive force","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Electromotive force, abbreviated as emf, is the amount of energy provided by the battery per unit charge to move round the complete circuit."}]]},{"title":"The internal resistance of a battery depends on its (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"size,  ဘက်ထရီအရွယ်အစား"}],[{"text":"chemical properties,  ဓာတုဗေဒဆိုင်ရာဂုဏ်သတ္တိ"}],[{"text":"life time, and  ဘက်ထရီ၏သက်တမ်း"}],[{"text":"temperature.  ဘက်ထရီကို အသုံးပြုခြင်းကြောင့် ဖြစ်ပေါ်လာသည့်အပူချိန်"}]]},{"title":"* Available voltage of the battery (Terminal voltage of the battery)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The potential difference across the terminals of a battery connected to an external circuit is called the terminal voltage (or available voltage) of the battery."}]]},{"title":"Charging a battery","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Charging a battery means the chemical energy of the battery which has been used up is now supplied back by electrical energy from some external source (battery charger). "}]]},{"title":"* Electrical energy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Electrical energy is energy associated with the flow of charge through any part of circuit."}],[{"text":"SI unit of electrical energy is joule (J). practical unit is kilowatt hour (kWh)."}]]},{"title":"* Electrical power","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Electrical power is the rate of transfer of electrical energy."}],[{"text":"SI unit of electrical power is watt (W)."}]]},{"title":"1 watt (W) of electrical power","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If 1 joule (J) of electrical energy is transferred in 1 second (s), the electrical power is 1 joule per second (J s⁻¹) or 1 watt (W)."}]]},{"title":"* Joule’s law of electricity and heat","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The amount of heat produced in a resistor due to a current flowing through it is directly proportional to the square of the current, the value of resistance and the time taken by the current to pass through the resistor."}],[{"text":" H = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{I^{2} R t}{J}","plain":"(I² R t)/(J)"},{"text":" Where, H = amount of heat"}],[{"text":"    I  = current"}],[{"text":"    R = resistance of the resistor"}],[{"text":"    t  = time taken"}],[{"text":"    J = Joule’s mechanical equivalent of heat (J = 4.2 J cal⁻¹)"}],[{"text":"If current is doubled, amount of heat produced will be 4 times larger."}]]},{"title":"* Fuse","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A fuse is a thin short wire of tin-lead alloy which is used in electric circuits to prevent electrical appliances from being damage if excessive current flows through them."}],[{"text":" - fuse    tin-lead ally  low melting point"}],[{"text":" - heating coil  nichrome wire  high melting point"}],[{"text":" - filament in bulb tungsten wire  1800"},{"math":true,"tex":"℃","plain":"℃"},{"text":" - produce white light"}],[{"text":"        2500"},{"math":true,"tex":"℃","plain":"℃"},{"text":" - used safely"}],[{"text":"        3000"},{"math":true,"tex":"℃","plain":"℃"},{"text":" - melting point"}]]}],[{"title":"Direct current (DC)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The direct current is a steady unidirectional current. In a direct current the electrons flow steadily in the same direction all the time."}]]},{"title":"Alternating current","paragraphs":[[{"text":" An alternating current is a current that reverses its direction at regular time intervals. In the alternating current the direction of the flow of electrons changes periodically."}]]},{"title":"Frequency of the emf","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The frequency of the emf, is the number of complete cycles generated in 1s."}]]},{"title":"AC generator","paragraphs":[[{"text":" An AC generator is a device for generating alternating current."}]]},{"title":"* Electromagnetic induction","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A current flows through a loop of wire whenever there is a change in the magnetic lines of force trough the loop. This phenomenon is called electromagnetic induction."}],[{"text":"One of the useful applications of electromagnetic induction is an induction coil."}],[{"text":"Induction coil can be used as a sparking plug of motor car. DC ဗို့အားကို များစေတယ်"}]]},{"title":"Induced current","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A current flows through a loop of wire whenever there is a change in the magnetic lines of force trough the loop. This current is called an induced current."}]]},{"title":"Induced emf","paragraphs":[[{"text":"The electromotive force (emf) which causes the induced current to flow is called an induced emf."}]]},{"title":"Magnetic flux density or magnetic induction B","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The magnetic flux density or magnetic induction B is the number of magnetic lines of force passing perpendicularly through a unit area of material."}],[{"text":"9. Magnetic flux "},{"math":true,"tex":"ϕ_{B}","plain":"ϕB"}],[{"text":" Magnetic flux "},{"math":true,"tex":"ϕ_{B}","plain":"ϕB"},{"text":" is a measurement of the total magnetic lines of force which passes through a surface area."}],[{"text":"Magnetic parameters | SI unit"}],[{"text":"Magnetic flux density, B | tesla (T) | Vector"}],[{"text":"Magnetic field strength, H | ampere per metre (A m-1) | Vector"}],[{"text":"Magnetic flux, "},{"math":true,"tex":"ϕ","plain":"ϕ"},{"text":" | weber (Wb) | Scalar"}]]},{"title":"Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction (2024, 2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The magnitude of induced emf in a wire loop is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux through that loop."}]]},{"title":"Lenz’s law of electromagnetic induction","paragraphs":[[{"text":" An induced emf is always in a direction that opposes the change in the original magnetic flux that causes it."}]]},{"title":"AC Generator","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A generator is an electromagnetic device which converts mechanical energy into electrical energy."}]]},{"title":"Transformer","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A transformer is an inductive electrical device that used to change the voltage of an alternating current."}]]},{"title":"Lamination","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The core of a transformer is built up of thin iron sheets of high resistance, called lamination."}]]},{"title":"Primary coil of transformer (P)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The coil of transformer connected to an alternating voltage source is called the primary coil and is denoted by P."}]]},{"title":"Secondary coil of transformer (S)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The coil of transformer connected to an electrical device to which electrical power to be supplied is called the secondary coil and is denoted by S."}]]},{"title":"Step-up transformer","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the secondary has more turns than the primary, the voltage obtained from the secondary is greater than the voltage applied to the primary. Such a transformer is called a step-up transformer."}],[{"text":"Ns > Np Vs > Vp Is < Ip"}]]},{"title":"Step-down transformer","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the secondary has fewer turns than the primary, the voltage obtained from the secondary is smaller than the voltage applied to the primary. Such a transformer is called a step-down transformer."}],[{"text":"Ns < Np Vs < Vp Is > Ip"}]]},{"title":"* Eddy current","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The currents flow in little circles in the iron core and are called eddy currents."}]]},{"title":"Efficiency of a transformer","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The efficiency of a transformer is the ratio of the output power to the input power as usual."}],[{"text":" Efficiency of a transformer = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{Output power}{Input power} \\times 100\\%","plain":"(Output power)/(Input power) ×100%"}],[{"text":"There is no ideal transformer with 100"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\%","plain":"%"},{"text":" efficiency."}],[{"text":"Most of the transformers have full load efficiency from 95"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\%","plain":"%"},{"text":" to 98.5"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\%","plain":"%"},{"text":"."}]]},{"title":"Circuit breaker","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A circuit breaker is an automatic switch which trips (turns OFF) when the current rises above the specified value."}],[{"text":"The voltage of live wire goes alternately negative and positive, making the current flow backwards and forwards through the circuit."}],[{"text":"Neutral wire completes the circuit. It is kept at zero voltage by the electricity supply company. "}],[{"text":"Earth wire is a safety wire. It connects the metal body of the appliance to earth and stops it becoming live. If the live wire comes loose and touches the metal body, a current immediately flows to earth and blows the fuse. "}]]},{"title":"Colour coding for wires","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Live wire Brown Red + fuse and switch"}],[{"text":" Neutral wire Blue Black"}],[{"text":" Earth wire  striped Green-yellow (new) Green (old)"}],[{"text":"Lighting circuit, Power circuit, heating circuit"}]]}],[{"title":"* Electronic","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The branch of physics and technology concerned with the design of circuits using transistors, integrated circuits and microchips, and also with the behavior and movement of electrons in a semiconductor, conductor or vacuum is kwon as electronics."}]]},{"title":"Active electrical components (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Active electrical components are components that require a power source to operate."}],[{"text":"vacuum tubes, transistors, diodes, ICs (integrated Circuits), TRIACs (Three terminal AC switches), SCRs (Silicon Controlled Rectifiers), LEDs (Light Emitting Diodes), etc."}]]},{"title":"Passive electrical components (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Passive electrical components are capable of operating without an external power source."}],[{"text":"resistors, capacitors, inductors, transformers, LDR, transducer, etc."}]]},{"title":"Zener diode (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Zener diode is a heavily doped semiconductor (silicon or germanium) pn junction diode."}],[{"text":"Zener diode makes the current flow in the backward direction when reverse biased."}],[{"text":"A normal diode allows current to flow only in one direction while zener diode allows current to flow in both directions."}]]},{"title":"Avalanche breakdown (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Avalanche breakdown occurs at junctions which being lightly doped have wide depletion layers."}]]},{"title":"Zener breakdown (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Zener breakdown occurs at junctions which being heavily doped have narrow depletion layers."}]]},{"title":"LED","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A LED is a semiconductor light source that emits light when current flows through it."}],[{"text":"Advantages of LED (2025)"}],[{"text":"Very low voltage (1 to 2 V) and current (5 to 20 mA) are enough to drive the LED."}],[{"text":"Less power consumption (less than 150 mW)"}],[{"text":"Fast action and no warmup time. (The response time is about 10 ns)"}],[{"text":"Miniature in size and hence light weight."}],[{"text":"Long life span and ruggedness."}],[{"text":"More reliable, more efficient to use under cold temperature. "}],[{"text":"Disadvantages of LED (2025)"}],[{"text":"A slight excess in voltage or current can damage the device."}],[{"text":"The device is known to have a much wider bandwidth (wavelength spread) compared to the laser."}],[{"text":"The device temperature depends on the radiant output power and wavelength."}],[{"text":"Little effective in wide-area."}],[{"text":"The visible lights that an LED emits are usually orange, red, yellow, or green. "}],[{"text":"The commonly used invisible light for LED is infrared light."}],[{"text":"The LEDs are made from exotic semiconductor compounds such as "}],[{"text":"Gallium Arsenide (GaAs), "}],[{"text":"Gallium Phosphide (GaP), "}],[{"text":"Gallium Arsenide Phosphide (GaAsP), "}],[{"text":"Silicon Carbide (SiC) or "}],[{"text":"Gallium Indium Nitride (GaInN) "}],[{"text":" all mixed together at different ratios to produce a distinct wavelength of colour. "}],[{"text":"LEDs do not directly produce white light. Using a blue LED with a phosphor coating to convert blue light to white light by a process called fluorescence."}]]},{"title":"Photodiode (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A photodiode is a semiconductor device that converts light into electrical current. Photodiodes work in reverse biased mode."}],[{"text":"The current is generated when photons are absorbed in the photodiode."}]]},{"title":"Photocurrent","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Photocurrent is an electric current produced by electromagnetic radiation in the photoelectric effect, photovoltaic effect or photoconductivity."}]]},{"title":"Light Dependent Resistor (LDR)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A light dependent resistor (LDR) is a light controlled variable resistor. LDR consists of a disc of semiconductor material on its surface with two electrodes."}],[{"text":"In the dark or in dim light, the material of the disc has a relatively small number of free electrons in it. It becomes a poor conductor of electric current. Its resistance is quite high (~ MΩ) called dark resistance."}],[{"text":"In the bright light, more electrons escape from the atoms of the semiconductor. It becomes a good conductor. Its resistance is low (few hundred Ω). "}],[{"text":"As light level increases, the LDR resistance decreases."}]]},{"title":"Thermistor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A thermistor is a temperature sensor that exhibits a large change in resistance proportional to a change in temperature. It can therefore convert changes in temperature into changes in electric current."}],[{"text":"The NTC’s resistance decreases as the temperature increases, and vice versa."}],[{"text":"The PTC’s resistance increases as the temperature increases, and vice versa."}]]},{"title":"Liquid crystals (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Liquid crystals are a state of matter which has properties between those of conventional liquids and those of solid crystals."}]]},{"title":"Liquid crystal display (LCD)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A liquid crystal display (LCD) is a flat-panel display or other electronically modulated optical device that uses the light-modulating properties of liquid crystals combined with polarizers."}]]},{"title":"Polarizer (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A polarizer is an optical device that can convert an unpolarized light wave into a polarize light wave by blocking all other vibrations."}]]},{"title":"* Digital electronics","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Digital electronics is a field of electronics involving the study of digital signals and the engineering of devices that use or produce them. This is in contrast to analog electronics and analog signals."}]]},{"title":"IC (integrated circuit)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" An IC (integrated circuit), sometimes called a chip (or) microchip, is a semiconductor wafer on which thousands (or) millions of tiny resistors, capacitors and transistors are fabricated. "}]]},{"title":"Boolean algebra","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Boolean algebra is a branch of mathematics where variables can have only two possible values: false and true, (or) logic values 0 and 1."}],[{"text":"The basic operations in Boolean algebra are AND, OR, and NOT."}]]},{"title":"De Morgan's 1st Theorem (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"InputOutputAB"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{AB}","plain":"AB̅"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"text":"0011010111101110 The complement of two or more ANDed variables is equivalent to the OR of the complements of the individual variables. "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{AB}","plain":"AB̅"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"}],[{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"text":"X = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"text":"A"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":"B"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A B}","plain":"A B̅"},{"text":"ABX = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A B}","plain":"A B̅"},{"math":true,"tex":"≡","plain":"≡"}]]},{"title":"De Morgan's 2nd Theorem (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"InputOutputAB"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A+B}","plain":"A+B̅"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"text":"0011010110001000 The complement of two or more ORed variables is equivalent to the AND of the complements of the individual variables. "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A+ B}","plain":"A+ B̅"},{"text":" = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"}],[{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A+ B}","plain":"A+ B̅"},{"text":"ABX = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A+ B}","plain":"A+ B̅"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"text":"X = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"text":"A"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{A}","plain":"A̅"},{"text":"B"},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{B}","plain":"B̅"},{"math":true,"tex":"≡","plain":"≡"}]]},{"title":"Fire alarm system (2024, 2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"QPABAlarmExtinguisherSmoke detectorHeat detector The fire alarm system consists of smoke detector, heat detector and two logic gates. The fire alarm will turn on when either smoke or heat is detected. If both smoke and heat are detected, the fire extinguisher will also be set to operate."}],[{"text":"ABP = A +BQ = AB0011010101110001 "}]]},{"title":"Street lighting system (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"SwitchLight sensor Street light001101011011Switch Light sensor Street light ON = 1OFF = 0Dark = 0Bright = 1ON = 1OFF = 0 A system is required which allows the street lights either to be turned on manually by a switch at any time or automatically by a light sensor when it is dark. The OR gate can be used since it gives a 1 output when either one or both of its inputs are 1."}]]},{"title":"Transduction","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The process of converting energy from one form to another is known as transduction. "}]]},{"title":"Sensor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Sensor is a device which produces a measurable response due to a change in physical conditions. "}],[{"text":"Eg. LDR – light sensor, thermistor – temperature sensor,"}]]},{"title":"Transducer (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In general, a transducer is a device that transforms a signal from one energy form to another energy form."}],[{"text":"Eg. loudspeakers, microphones, oximeter, thermometers, LEDs and antenna."}]]},{"title":"* Modulation     ညှိခြင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The process of changing some characteristics (e.g. amplitude, frequency or phase) of a carrier wave (e.g. radio wave) in accordance with the intensity of the signal is known as modulation."}],[{"text":"Types of modulation There are three basic types of modulation: "}],[{"text":"(i) Amplitude modulation (AM),  လွှဲကျယ် ညှိခြင်း "}],[{"text":"(ii) Frequency modulation (FM) and  ကြိမ်နှုန်း ညှိခြင်း"}],[{"text":"(iii) Phase modulation (PM). အသွင် ညှိခြင်း"}]]},{"title":"Amplitude Modulation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" When the amplitude of high frequency carrier wave is changed in accordance with the intensity of the signal, it is called amplitude modulation. "}]]},{"title":"Frequency Modulation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" When the frequency of carrier wave is changed in accordance with the intensity of the signal, it is called frequency modulation (FM)."}]]},{"title":"Phase Modulation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Phase modulation is a modulation pattern that encodes information as variations in the instantaneous phase of a carrier wave."}],[{"text":"Table- The comparison of frequency modulation (FM) and amplitude modulation (AM) "}],[{"text":"FM | AM"}],[{"text":"(i) | The amplitude of carrier wave remains constant with modulation. | (i) | The amplitude of carrier wave changes with modulation."}],[{"text":"(ii) | The carrier frequency changes according to the strength of the modulating signal. | (ii) | The carrier amplitude changes according to the strength of the modulating signal."}],[{"text":"(iii) | The carrier frequency changes with modulation. | (iii) | The carrier frequency remains constant with modulation."}],[{"text":"(iv) | FM radio ranges in a higher spectrum from 88 to 108 MHz. (or) 1200 to 2400 bits per second. | (iv) | AM radio ranges from 535 to 1705 k Hz (or) up to 1200 bits per second."}]]},{"title":"* Demodulation","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The process of recovering the audio signal from the modulated wave is known as demodulation or detection."}]]},{"title":"Demodulator","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A demodulator is an electronic circuit (or computer program in a software defined radio) that is used to recover the information content from the modulated carrier wave."}]]},{"title":"Simple Radio Receiver","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The radio receiver comprises roughly six pieces. They are antenna, tuner, rf (radio frequency) amplifier, diode detector, af (audio frequency) amplifier and speaker."}]]}],[{"title":"* Radioactivity","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The emission of some or all of alpha, beta and gamma rays from the nucleus of an unstable atom is called radioactivity."}]]},{"title":"Alpha decay","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Alpha decay ("},{"math":true,"tex":"\\alpha ","plain":"α"},{"text":" decay) is a process in which an atomic nucleus emits an alpha particle (helium nucleus) and transforms (or decays) into a different atomic nucleus, with mass number reduces by four and atomic number reduces by two as described by equation."}],[{"text":"In symbols,         "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"Z-2A-4Y","plain":"Z-2A-4Y"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"24He","plain":"24He"}],[{"text":" Where,  "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" = parent nucleus, "}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"Z-2A-4Y","plain":"Z-2A-4Y"},{"text":" = daughter nucleus,"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"24He","plain":"24He"},{"text":" = alpha particle"}],[{"text":"  An example of alpha decay is   "},{"math":true,"tex":"92238U","plain":"92238U"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"90234Th","plain":"90234Th"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"24He","plain":"24He"}]]},{"title":"β⁻ decay","paragraphs":[[{"text":" β⁻ decay (beta-minus decay) is a process in which a neutron in an atomic nucleus changes to a proton with the emission of an electron (β⁻) and an antineutrino ("},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{e}","plain":"ν̅e"},{"text":"). The parent nucleus transforms into the daughter nucleus, with mass number remains unchanged but atomic number increases by one."}],[{"text":" In symbols,          "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"Z+1AY","plain":"Z+1AY"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"-10e","plain":"-10e"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{e}","plain":"ν̅e"}],[{"text":" Where,  "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" = parent nucleus,"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"Z+1AY","plain":"Z+1AY"},{"text":" = daughter nucleus, "}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"-10e","plain":"-10e"},{"text":" = electron (β⁻ particle)"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{e}","plain":"ν̅e"},{"text":" = antineutrino"}],[{"text":"  An example of β⁻ decay is  "},{"math":true,"tex":"614C","plain":"614C"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"714N","plain":"714N"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"-10e","plain":"-10e"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{e}","plain":"ν̅e"}]]},{"title":"β⁺ decay","paragraphs":[[{"text":" β⁺ decay (beta-plus decay) is a process in which a proton in an atomic nucleus changes to a neutron with the emission of a positron (β⁺) and a neutrino ("},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{e}","plain":"νe"},{"text":"). The parent nucleus transforms into the daughter nucleus, with mass number remains unchanged but atomic number decreases by one."}],[{"text":" In symbols,          "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"Z-1AY","plain":"Z-1AY"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"+10e","plain":"+10e"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{e}","plain":"νe"}],[{"text":" Where,  "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" = parent nucleus,"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"Z-1AY","plain":"Z-1AY"},{"text":" = daughter nucleus, "}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"+10e","plain":"+10e"},{"text":" = positron (β⁺ particle)"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{e}","plain":"νe"},{"text":" = neutrino"}],[{"text":"  An example of β⁺ decay is  "},{"math":true,"tex":"611C","plain":"611C"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"511B","plain":"511B"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"+10e","plain":"+10e"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{e}","plain":"νe"}]]},{"title":"Positron","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Positron, the antiparticle of electron, has the same mass and same magnitude of electric charge as an electron; but positron is positively charged. "}]]},{"title":"Neutrino","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Neutrino, a fundamental particle as an electron, has no charge and negligibly small rest mass "},{"math":true,"tex":"(<2\\times 10^{-37}kg","plain":"(<2×10⁻³⁷kg"},{"text":"). "}],[{"text":"There are three types of neutrinos; called "}],[{"text":"electron neutrino ("},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{e}","plain":"νe"},{"text":"),"}],[{"text":"the muon neutrino ("},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{μ}","plain":"νμ"},{"text":") "}],[{"text":"the tau neutrino ("},{"math":true,"tex":"ν_{\\tau }","plain":"ντ"},{"text":"). "}],[{"text":"Each neutrino is associated with an antineutrino: "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{e}","plain":"ν̅e"},{"text":", "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{μ}","plain":"ν̅μ"},{"text":", "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{\\tau }","plain":"ν̅τ"},{"text":"."}]]},{"title":"Gamma decay","paragraphs":[[{"text":" In gamma decay (γ decay), the daughter nucleus resulting from alpha or beta decay is left in an excited state and it can then decay to a lower energy state by emitting a gamma ray photon. In gamma decay, the atomic number and mass number of the parent and daughter nuclei remain unchanged; only the parent nucleus releases energy in the form of electromagnetic radiation."}],[{"text":" In symbols,          "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX^{*}","plain":"ZAX^(*)"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"00γ","plain":"00γ"}],[{"text":" Where,  "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX^{*}","plain":"ZAX^(*)"},{"text":" = parent nucleus (excited state),"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"ZAX","plain":"ZAX"},{"text":" = daughter nucleus (ground state),"}],[{"text":"   "},{"math":true,"tex":"00γ","plain":"00γ"},{"text":" = gamma photon"}],[{"text":"For example, cobalt-60 undergoes β- emission to an excited state of nickle-60, which in turn decays to the ground state by "},{"math":true,"tex":"γ","plain":"γ"},{"text":" emission."}],[{"text":"  "},{"math":true,"tex":"2760Co","plain":"2760Co"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"2860Ni^{*}","plain":"2860Ni^(*)"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"-10e","plain":"-10e"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\bar{ν}_{e}","plain":"ν̅e"},{"text":" ,  "},{"math":true,"tex":"2860Ni^{*}","plain":"2860Ni^(*)"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\to ","plain":"→"},{"text":" "},{"math":true,"tex":"2860Ni","plain":"2860Ni"},{"text":" + "},{"math":true,"tex":"00γ","plain":"00γ"}]]},{"title":"Photon","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The photon is the quantum of the electromagnetic field including electromagnetic radiation such as light, X-rays and gamma rays."}],[{"text":"(i) Properties of alpha rays"}],[{"text":"Alpha rays consist of helium nuclei, positively charged particles. An "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\alpha ","plain":"α"},{"text":" particle consists of two protons and two neutrons. The charge of an "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\alpha ","plain":"α"},{"text":" particle is "},{"math":true,"tex":"+2e (+ 3.2\\times 10^{-19}C).","plain":"+2e (+ 3.2×10⁻¹⁹C)."}],[{"text":"Alpha rays can be deflected by electric and magnetic fields."}],[{"text":"Alpha rays are the most ionizing of the three rays."}],[{"text":"Alpha rays are the least penetrating of the three rays."}],[{"text":"Alpha rays can be stopped by a thick sheet of paper."}],[{"text":"(ii) Properties of beta rays"}],[{"text":"Beta rays consists of either electrons (charge = - e) or positrons (charge = "},{"math":true,"tex":"+ e)","plain":"+ e)"},{"text":" moving with varying speeds."}],[{"text":"Beta rays can be deflected by electric and magnetic fields."}],[{"text":"Beta rays are less ionizing than alpha rays."}],[{"text":"Beta rays are more penetrating than alpha rays."}],[{"text":"Beta rays can be stopped by a few millimetres of aluminium."}],[{"text":"(iii) Properties of gamma rays (2024)"}],[{"text":"Gamma rays are electromagnetic waves like light and X-ray, but have much shorter wavelengths. Gamma rays are high energy photons. Gamma rays have no charge."}],[{"text":"Gamma rays cannot be deflected by electric and magnetic fields."}],[{"text":"Gamma rays are the least ionizing of the three rays."}],[{"text":"Gamma rays are the most penetrating of the three rays."}],[{"text":"Intensity of gamma rays can be greatly reduced by several centimetres of lead but they are never completely stopped."}]]},{"title":"Activity (2025)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The rate of decay of a radioactive sample is called activity."}],[{"text":"The SI unit of activity is becquerel (Bq)."}],[{"text":"1 Bq = 1 event s⁻¹ (or 1 disintegration s⁻¹)"}],[{"text":"Units larger than Bq are MBq (10⁶ Bq) and GBq (10⁹ Bq)"}],[{"text":"A unit of activity still being used today is curie (Ci)."}],[{"text":"1 Ci = 3.7 "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\times  10^{10} ","plain":"× 10¹⁰ "},{"text":"event s⁻¹ = 37 GBq"}],[{"text":"The rate of decay (activity) is a characteristic of the radioactive element and it is unaffected by the temperature. "}],[{"text":"Activity of a radioactive sample decreases with time."}]]},{"title":"* Half-life","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The half-life is defined as the time for half the atoms in a radioactive sample to decay."}]]},{"title":"Radioisotope","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A radioisotope (radioactive isotope or radionuclide) is an atom with an unstable nucleus which undergoes radioactive decay, resulting in the emission of alpha, beta and gamma rays."}]]},{"title":"Tracer","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A tracer is a small amount of radioactive isotope introduced into a system in order to follow the behaviour of some components of that system."}]]},{"title":"Radiotherapy","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Treatment with radiation is known as radiotherapy."}]]},{"title":"Radioactive dating","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Radioactive dating is a technique for estimating the age of an object by measuring the amounts of various radioisotopes present in it."}]]},{"title":"Carbon dating","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Carbon dating is a technique for estimating the age of the remains of plants and animals by measuring the activity of carbon-14 presents in the remains."}],[{"text":"Carbon is present in the atmosphere and also in the bodies of plants and animals."}],[{"text":"Half-life of carbon-14 is 5730 years."}],[{"text":"High energy neutrons of cosmic rays bombard nitrogen-14 atoms and produce carbon-14 atoms."}],[{"text":"Abundance of carbon-14 in the atmosphere is essentially constant."}],[{"text":"The amount of carbon-14 present in the living body is essentially constant."}],[{"text":"There is one carbon-14 atom in every trillion (10¹²) of carbon-12 atoms."}],[{"text":"When an animal or a plant dies carbon cycle stops and the carbon-14 in the remain gradually decreases by radioactive decay."}],[{"text":"potassium-40 (radioactive) can be used to estimate the age of the igneous rocks."}],[{"text":"Igneous rocks can also be dated by the proportion of uranium to lead isotopes."}],[{"text":"Lead is the stable final decay product of a series of decays that starts with uranium."}],[{"text":" Gamma and neutron irradiation of seeds can be used to induce mutations to "}],[{"text":"develop desired mutants that are resistant to disease,"}],[{"text":"produce a higher yield and "}],[{"text":"allow earlier ripening."}],[{"text":" Radiation can also be used to "}],[{"text":"destroy microbes in food and "}],[{"text":"control insect and parasite infestation in harvested food."}]]},{"title":"* Nuclear reaction (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" A nuclear reaction is a process in which a particle or a gamma radiation penetrate into a nucleus and changes its configuration. "}]]},{"title":"Exothermic reaction","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the total mass before a nuclear reaction takes place is greater than the total mass after reaction, this mass defect is released as energy in a nuclear reaction according to Einstein’s relation E = m c². This type of reaction is known as exothermic reaction."}]]},{"title":"Endothermic reaction","paragraphs":[[{"text":" If the total mass before a nuclear reaction takes place is less than the total mass after reaction, external energy must be provided for the reaction to take place. This type of reaction is known as endothermic reaction."}]]},{"title":"Nuclear reactor","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Nuclear reactor is a device in which a fission chain reaction can be initiated, maintained and controlled."}],[{"text":"The essential elements of a nuclear reactor are fissionable nuclear fuel, moderator, coolant, control rods, reactor vessel and shielding."}],[{"text":"Fissionable nuclear fuel : is the material for fission reaction to take place. Uranium-235 or plutonium-239 can be used as fuel."}],[{"text":"Moderator : Neutrons released from a fission reaction are fast neutrons. They need to be slowed down to maintain a chain reaction. Moderator is a material to slow down fast neutrons released from a fission process. Graphite, ordinary water (H₂O) and heavy water (D₂O) are used as moderators."}],[{"text":"Coolant  : is to extract the heat produced by fission fragments when they slow down and stop in the reactor material. Water and carbon dioxide CO₂ gas are used as coolant."}],[{"text":"Control rods : are used to control the rate of reaction by raising or lowering them from the core. Boron and cadmium (neutron absorbing materials) are used as control rods. "}],[{"text":"Reactor vessel  : is to contain the reactor core, moderator, coolant and control rods. It is a pressurized steel vessel."}],[{"text":"Shielding  : (or containment structure) is a protective barrier, usually a dense material. It reduces the passage of radiations from radioactive materials by absorbing them."}]]},{"title":"Radioactive waste ရေဒီယိုသတ္တိကြွစွန့်ပစ်ပစ္စည်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Materials which are radioactive and for which there is no further use are called radioactive waste."}],[{"text":"Strontium-90 becomes concentrated in bones."}],[{"text":"Iodine-131 becomes concentrated in the thyroid gland. "}],[{"text":"Plutonium-239 is itself a nuclear fuel and is also used in nuclear weapons. It is highly toxic. If breathed in, a very small amount can kill a person."}],[{"text":"Atomic bomb - uncontrolled chain. Nuclear reactor - controlled chain reaction"}],[{"text":"The first atomic bomb (Little Boy), two lumps of pure uranium-235."}],[{"text":"In present day nuclear weapons, plutonium-239 is used for fission."}]]},{"title":"* de Broglie wavelength","paragraphs":[[{"text":" The wavelength λ associated with a moving particle (matter wave) is also called de Broglie wavelength."}]]},{"title":"1 eV (electron volt)","paragraphs":[[{"text":" 1 eV (electron volt) is the energy gained by an electron (a particle of charge e) when accelerated through a potential of one volt."}]]},{"title":"Einstein special theory of relativity is based on two simple postulates","paragraphs":[[{"text":"The laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames of reference."}],[{"text":"The speed of light in vacuum is the same in all inertial frames of reference and is not affected by the speed of its source."}]]},{"title":"* Inertial frame of reference","paragraphs":[[{"text":" An inertial frame is a frame of reference that has no acceleration where all objects follow Newton's first law of motion. "}]]},{"title":"The consequences of Einstein special theory of relativity","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Relativity of simultaneity.","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Relativistic effects on time interval (time dilation).","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Relativistic effects on length (length contraction).","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Relativistic effects on mass, momentum and energy.","paragraphs":[]},{"title":"Relativity of simultaneity","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Relativity of simultaneity states that two events that are simultaneous in one frame of reference may not be simultaneous in another fame moving relative to the first frame."}]]},{"title":"* Time dilation     အချိန်ဆန့်ခြင်း","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Time dilation is the phenomenon of time passing more slowly for an observer who is moving relative to another observer."}],[{"text":"Time dilation - "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\Delta ","plain":"Δ"},{"text":" t = "},{"math":true,"tex":"γ \\Delta t_{0}","plain":"γ Δt₀"},{"text":" အချိန်ဆန့််"}],[{"text":"Length contraction - L = "},{"math":true,"tex":"\\frac{L_{0}}{γ}","plain":"(L₀)/(γ)"},{"text":" အလျားတို"}],[{"text":"Relativistic effect on mass - m = "},{"math":true,"tex":"γ","plain":"γ"},{"text":" m₀ ဒြပ်ထုများ"}]]},{"title":"Rest mass","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Rest mass is the mass of a particle measured when it is at rest relative to the observer."}],[{"text":"Non-zero rest mass particle  - proton, neutron, electron"}],[{"text":"Zero rest mass particle - photon"}],[{"text":"Negligibly small rest mass particle - neutrino"}]]},{"title":"Material particle","paragraphs":[[{"text":" Material particle is a particle that has nonzero rest mass, like proton, neutron and electron."}]]},{"title":"Comparison between Einstein’s special and general relativity theories (2024)","paragraphs":[[{"text":"Special Relativity | General Relativity"}],[{"text":"Published in 1905 | Final form published in 1916"}],[{"text":"A theory of space-time | A theory of gravity"}],[{"text":"Applies to observers moving at constant speed (inertial frame) | Applies to observers that are accelerating (non-inertial frame)"}],[{"text":"Most useful in the field of nuclear physics | Most useful in the field of astrophysics"}],[{"text":"Accepted quickly and put to practical use by nuclear physicists and quantum chemists | Largely ignored until 1960 when new mathematical techniques made the theory more accessible and astronomers found some important applications"}],[{"text":"နျူကလီယရူပဗေဒ ပညာရှင်များနှင့် ကွမ်တမ်ဓာတ်တု ပညာရှင်များက ချက်ချင်းပင်လက်ခံကာ လက်တွေ့ အသုံးပြုခဲ့သည်။ | နက္ခတ္တပညာရှင်များသည် အရေးပါသည့် အသုံးပြုချက် များကိုမတွေ့သေးမှီ၊ သီအိုရီကို လက်လှမ်းမှီနိုင်မည့် သင်္ချာနည်းပညာသစ်များမပေါ်သေးမှီ 1690 မတိုင်မှီ အထိ လက်မခံဘဲ ငြင်းဆိုခဲ့ကြသည်။"}],[{"text":"Also note that the theory of general relativity includes the theory of special relativity."}],[{"text":"Sun - nuclear fusion reaction/ nuclear power plants - nuclear fission reactions."}],[{"text":"Decay of radioactive nuclei are good examples of conversion of mass into energy."}]]}]];
